<h2>Accuracy: Which One Catches More Errors?</h2>
<p>At the sentence level, Grammarly and ProWritingAid both catch the vast majority of grammar, spelling, and punctuation errors. Grammarly's advantage is <strong>context</strong> — its suggestions explain <em>why</em> a fix is better and adapt to your tone and audience. ProWritingAid's advantage is <strong>depth</strong> — it catches structural issues (repeated words, pacing problems, passive voice patterns, sticky sentences) that no sentence-level checker can see. QuillBot catches common errors well but is less context-aware, which is fine for basic editing but not for serious writing.</p>

<h2>AI Features in 2026</h2>
<p>All three tools have added AI capabilities, but they take different approaches:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Grammarly:</strong> full AI writing assistant — generate text from a prompt, rewrite paragraphs, adjust tone, and summarize documents. Deeply integrated with Superhuman Mail and Coda.</li>
<li><strong>ProWritingAid:</strong> AI Sparks offer rewriting and improvement suggestions tied to its 25+ reports. Premium Pro unlocks 50 Sparks/day and AI critiques.</li>
<li><strong>QuillBot:</strong> no standalone AI generator; its AI power is focused on the paraphraser's understanding of context and meaning.</li>
</ul>

