<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Is Grammarly better than QuillBot?</h3>
<p>Grammarly is better for everyday professional writing across apps — email, Slack, Google Docs, browsers — with context-aware suggestions, tone detection, and AI writing features. QuillBot is better if you mainly need paraphrasing and rewriting at a lower price. For most business users, Grammarly is the more complete tool.</p>

<h3>Is ProWritingAid better than Grammarly?</h3>
<p>ProWritingAid is better for fiction authors and long-form manuscript writers — it offers 25+ in-depth reports (pacing, dialogue, sticky sentences, echoes) that Grammarly doesn't have, plus Scrivener integration and a lifetime license option. Grammarly is better for everyday cross-app writing and business communication.</p>

<h3>Which is the cheapest grammar checker?</h3>
<p>QuillBot Premium is the cheapest at $9.95/month (about $4.17/month billed annually). ProWritingAid Premium costs $30/month or $120/year (~$10/month). Grammarly Pro costs $12/month billed annually ($144/year). For long-term value, ProWritingAid also offers a one-time lifetime license ($399).</p>

<h3>Can I use these grammar checkers for free?</h3>
<p>Yes, all three have free tiers. Grammarly's free plan covers basic spelling, grammar, and punctuation with unlimited checks. QuillBot's free tier includes limited paraphrasing and grammar checks. ProWritingAid's free plan allows 500 words per check with 3 AI Sparks per day. Free plans are fine for light use; heavy writers need a paid tier.</p>

<h3>Which grammar checker is best for academic writing?</h3>
<p>ProWritingAid is the strongest for academic writing because its reports analyze structure, transitions, repetition, and readability — things students and researchers need. Grammarly Pro includes plagiarism detection and citation support, which also helps academic work. QuillBot is useful for paraphrasing sources but is not purpose-built for academic manuscripts.</p>

<h3>Should I use Grammarly and ProWritingAid together?</h3>
<p>Many authors and career writers run both: ProWritingAid for deep manuscript analysis (pacing, structure, style reports) and Grammarly for fast, cross-app proofreading in email, Slack, and browsers. Combined cost is roughly $22/month with annual billing. Casual writers rarely need both.</p>

<h2>Final Verdict</h2>
<p>There's no single "best" grammar checker in 2026 — the right tool depends on what you write:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Most people:</strong> <a href="/tool/grammarly">Grammarly</a> — the best all-rounder for everyday professional writing</li>
<li><strong>Authors & academics:</strong> <a href="https://prowritingaid.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ProWritingAid</a> — unmatched depth for long-form work</li>
<li><strong>Budget & paraphrasing:</strong> <a href="/tool/quillbot">QuillBot</a> — the cheapest solid option</li>
<li><strong>Serious writers:</strong> run Grammarly + ProWritingAid together</li>
</ul>
<p>Start with the free tiers of all three, try them on your actual writing, and upgrade when one proves it saves you time. For most professionals, Grammarly's cross-app convenience wins. For authors, ProWritingAid's depth is worth the learning curve.</p>

